```
Reason:
The first statement uses a single mark `MARK_DIFF_ENCODE` for both states currently being checked and states already fully verified, causing loops in the current chain to be mistaken for already-verified chains. The second statement incorrectly compares the inner loop iterators `j` and `k` instead of using the outer loop index `i`, allowing backward jumps within the same chain to be considered safe, which circumvents loop detection.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
((BASE_TABLE(dfa)[j] & MATCH_FLAG_DIFF_ENCODE) &&
 !(BASE_TABLE(dfa)[j] & MARK_DIFF_ENCODE_VERIFIED));
```
```c
if (BASE_TABLE(dfa)[j] & MARK_DIFF_ENCODE)
    /* loop in current chain */
    goto out;
```
```c
for (j = i;
     (BASE_TABLE(dfa)[j] & MATCH_FLAG_DIFF_ENCODE);
     j = k) {
    k = DEFAULT_TABLE(dfa)[j];
    if (j < i)
        break;
    BASE_TABLE(dfa)[j] &= ~MARK_DIFF_ENCODE;
    BASE_TABLE(dfa)[j] |= MARK_DIFF_ENCODE_VERIFIED;
}
```
Reason:
The first change introduces a separate mark `MARK_DIFF_ENCODE_VERIFIED` for states that have been fully verified, preventing confusion with the current chain. The second change detects loops in the current chain by checking for the temporary mark `MARK_DIFF_ENCODE` already set during traversal. The third change adds a second loop to move marks from temporary to verified after exiting the chain, and correctly uses `j < i` to skip already verified chains (based on outer loop index), removing the erroneous backward check.

